INT. ASTEROID MINING SKIFF "TALLOW-4" - COCKPIT - CONTINUOUS

A cramped steel bubble, instrument lights glowing amber. Beyond the canopy, a tumbling field of black rock, each boulder glinting where the sun hits an ore seam. A coffee bulb floats beside a headrest, trembling.

A lone figure in a patched flight suit. TAMARA OKONKWO-HESS (40s, shaved head, calm). She pilots with one hand, adjusts a thruster dial with the other, and scans the radar, with her boot against the bulkhead for balance and her eyes tracking three rocks at once.

A beep. A proximity warning. She taps it away without looking.

TAMARA
Easy, girl.

She nudges the stick. The skiff drifts left, grazing a boulder that spins lazily past.

The radio chirps. A thin voice, hers, recorded and looped, comes through the speaker.

PILOT (V.O.)
(recording)
Tallow Four, Control. Say status. Say status.

Tamara snorts and silences the loop with a flick of a switch.

TAMARA
Status is lonely.

Another beep. Another rock, the size of a house, looms ahead. She bites her lip, rolls the skiff, and slips through the gap with a hand's breadth to spare.

PILOT (V.O.)
(recording)
Tallow Four, you are out of range. Return to course. Return to course.

TAMARA
(softly, to the canopy)
There is no course.

She switches the loop off for good. The cockpit is silent save for the hiss of air. Out ahead, the field opens, and the sun lights a long vein of silver in a distant rock.
